 "literal": "The beautiful-striped young pup of a small white snake / having afflicted a forest elephant, in that manner / she who is young, she of the sprout-like bright teeth, / she of the bangled hands, has afflicted me.",
 "translation": "Just as the young pup of a small, white, beautifully-striped snake torments a massive forest elephant, so has she—young, with bright, sprout-like teeth and bangled hands—tormented me.",
 "modern": "ஒரு சிறிய, வெண்மையான, அழகிய வரிகளையுடைய பாம்பின் குட்டியானது காட்டில் வாழும் பெரிய யானையை வருத்துவது போல, இளமையானவளும், முளை போன்ற ஒளிரும் பற்களை உடையவளும், வளையல்களை அணிந்த கைகளை உடையவளுமாகிய அவள் என்னை வருத்தினாள்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Kuriñci (mountainous) landscape, where the theme is the union of lovers and the intense feelings of first love. The speaker is the hero, who has just met the heroine and is overwhelmed by her beauty and the power she holds over him. He compares himself to a mighty forest elephant and the heroine to a tiny, young, beautifully striped white snake. Just as a small snake's presence can completely paralyze and distress a giant elephant, this young, delicate girl with bright, sprout-like teeth and bangled hands has completely captivated and tormented his heart. The contrast between the smallness of the snake and the greatness of the elephant highlights the overwhelming power of love. Key words include 'அணங்கி' (having afflicted) and 'குருளை' (young pup).",
